{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>If the men have come to call you, arise, go with them.</b> Now was it not already stated, 'You shall not go with them'? But it is like the matter of (Numbers 13) שלח לך אנשים — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'Send for your part' — I am not commanding you — because He had already said to them in Egypt (Exodus 3:16–17), אעלה אתכם מעני מצרים אל ארץ טובה ורחבה, and afterward He said to them (Deuteronomy 1) עלה רש, and they did not believe, but said (ibid.) נשלחה אנשים לפנינו ויחפרו לנו את הארץ, and the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to follow their opinion and said to Moses, שלח לך אנשים. So too here, the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Balaam לא תלך עמהם, and afterward said to him קום לך אתם — He wished to follow his opinion. From here you learn: in the way a person wants to go, they lead him." "<b>And there are those who interpret</b> that what He said, 'You shall not go with them,' means: on condition of cursing; and afterward He said, 'If the men have come to call you' — for calling alone, that you may go with them: קום לך אתם — on condition that you do not curse." "<b>But only the thing that I shall speak to you.</b> Even to tell you to bless — you shall bless them." "<b>And here</b> one should ask whether there was power in Balaam's word to harm or to benefit, or not. If you say there was, how is it possible that there be power in flesh and blood to change the decree of the Creator? And if the Creator decreed concerning Israel, as it is said, כי ברוך הוא, how can there be power in his curse to change what He has already decreed? And if you say there was no substance to the curse at all, then why did the Holy One, blessed be He, restrain him and say to him, לא תאור את העם? Let Balaam curse all day long, so long as the Holy One, blessed be He, blesses, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 109): יקללו המה ואתה תברך. And the answer to this is that Balaam had no power in his word whatsoever, that his blessing or his curse should take effect. And the proof that there is no power in his word regarding a curse is what we find explicitly — that there is no power in his word regarding a blessing: for if there were power in his word in the matter of blessing, once it was clarified to him that the will of the Holy One, blessed be He, was not to curse Israel as Balak was commanding him, why did he not bless Balak and his people? And thereby the intention of Balak — that Israel should not prevail against him — would be fulfilled. Or why should he press himself for the hire of Balak's silver and gold? Let him bless himself to be a great king over all the kings, and he would not need his silver and gold. And we find explicitly that he did bless himself, saying (Numbers 23) תמות נפשי מות ישרים, and his blessing was not fulfilled, but his end was evil and his hope came to naught, for he died an unusual death, as it is written (Joshua 13) ואת בלעם בן בעור הקוסם הרגו בחרב. You thus learn that there was no power in his word to curse them — this is from the side of his speech; but from the side of his wisdom, in that he knew how to pinpoint the hour in which the Holy One, blessed be He, is angry, there was certainly power in his word to curse. Nevertheless, the question still stands in its place, for since the Holy One, blessed be He, was not angry on those days, it follows that all his words and wisdom were void, and his power failed from the side of both his speech and his wisdom — for behold, there is no power in his curse except at the time of anger, and since there was no time of anger, his wisdom was powerless. If so, why did He not let him curse, and say לא תאור את העם? The answer to this is that it was revealed and known before Him, blessed be He, the matter of the plague that was destined to come, and lest they say that because of Balaam's curse the plague came — therefore He restrained him from cursing. But certainly Balaam had no power from the side of his speech, neither in blessing nor in cursing — only from the side of his wisdom in pinpointing the hour in which the Holy One, blessed be He, is angry."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {3 "Citations like (במדבר יג) and (תהלים קט) given in Hebrew numeral shorthand; expanded to full chapter numbers as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:16.392345757Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}